# Flutter reusability

Proactively reuse established design-system patterns. Do **not** extract every widget “just in case.”

## Decision ladder

1. Use the stock Flutter/Material (or project library) control with the established theme.
2. Reuse an existing app shared component under `lib/core/ui/` (or the project's equivalent).
3. If the visual is clearly **app-wide** (branded header, common card shell, text field, button, dialog), extract or extend a small shared widget — even on first use.
4. Keep feature-specific / one-off visuals local under the feature folder; promote only when a second feature truly needs them.
5. Avoid “configurable everything” widgets — small, obvious parameter sets only.

## Rules

- Inspect the component library and theme before writing new UI.
- On a later screen with the same patterns, reuse — no duplicate app bars/cards/hardcoded style values.
- Do not wrap an unchanged stock `Card` / `AppBar` with no shared styling or behavior.
- Prefer composable widgets + design-system tokens + the project's current state management.
- Avoid huge monolith widgets **and** needless micro-fragmentation; balance readability.
- Same reuse mindset for validators/helpers used across features.

```text
lib/core/theme/
lib/core/ui/widgets/   # shared only when warranted
lib/features/<feature>/presentation/
```
